{
  "results": [
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_sources#4",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_sources",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data sources > 2. Emergency department visits: NSSP",
      "text": "- **Dataset:** NSSP Emergency Department Visit Trajectories by State and Sub State Regions - COVID-19, Flu, RSV, Combined.\n- **Publisher:** CDC, National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP).\n- **Link:** [rdmq-nq56](https://data.cdc.gov/Public-Health-Surveillance/NSSP-Emergency-Department-Visit-Trajectories-by-St/rdmq-nq56)\n- **Releases used:** <!-- auto:releases_nssp -->54<!-- /auto:releases_nssp --> dated downloads of the full file, the latest dated <!-- auto:newest_nssp -->2026-10-14<!-- /auto:newest_nssp -->.\n- **Fields used:** percent of ED visits (raw and smoothed) for COVID-19, influenza, RSV, and all three combined, plus CDC's trend label for each pathogen."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#7",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Emergency department visits",
      "text": "Summary:\n\n- Indicates whether the amount of people going to the emergency department for COVID-19, flu or RSV is very low, low, moderate, high, or very high.\n\nExplanation:\n\n- Emergency department data are collected through the National Syndromic Surveillance Program.\n- Activity levels are based on comparing the percent of emergency department visits for COVID-19, flu, or RSV visits during the current week to the average percent of visits when COVID-19, flu, or RSV visits were low, respectively.\n- These low points represent what are considered \u201cbaseline\u201d levels. The activity level is based on how elevated the current week is from the \u201cbaseline\u201d period. See Activity Levels data notes for the detailed methodology."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_faq#2",
      "doc_id": "resp_faq",
      "heading": "Release Notes and Frequently Asked Questions (Respiratory Illnesses Data Channel) > Understanding the data > Overall respiratory illness activity",
      "text": "Explanation:\n\n- Respiratory illness activity is monitored using the acute respiratory illness (ARI) definition. ARI captures a broad range of emergency department visits for respiratory illnesses, ranging from the common cold to potentially severe illnesses like flu (influenza), RSV and COVID-19. It captures a broader range of respiratory illnesses, including those that do not present with fever, compared to the previously shown influenza-like illness (ILI) metric (Note: the ILI metric is still being used for flu monitoring).\n- Respiratory illness activity levels are based on comparing the percent of emergency department visits for ARI during the current week to the average percent of visits during times when COVID-19, flu and RSV visits were low (Note: other respiratory illness-related visits may still be occurring). These low points are considered \"baseline\" levels of respiratory illness activity The activity level is based on how elevated the current week is from the \"baseline\" period. See Activity Levels data notes for the detailed methodology.\n- Emergency department data are collected through the National Syndromic Surveillance Program."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "resp_activity_levels#15",
      "doc_id": "resp_activity_levels",
      "heading": "Illness Levels Over Time > Data notes > Display criteria for sub-state levels (categories)",
      "text": "- To assess if an HSA meets the criteria for classification based on sufficient data, data weeks from Week 1 to Week 26 of 2026 were considered. If the total number of ED visits in an HSA during these weeks was equal to or greater than the corresponding V value, it indicated sufficient data. However, if the total number of weeks meeting this minimum count fell below 80% (21 out of 26 weeks), the level (category) was not displayed for that HSA in any week. The percentage of ED visits for COVID-19, influenza, or RSV may still be displayed even if the level (category) is not displayed.\n\n- **Sources**: COVID-19 and RSV: National Respiratory and Enteric Virus Surveillance System (NREVSS), a sentinel network of laboratories located through the US, includes clinical, public health and commercial laboratories. Flu: Clinical laboratory test results from NREVSS and flu collaborating laboratories; more details about influenza virologic surveillance are available."
    },
    {
      "chunk_id": "oad_data_dictionary#19",
      "doc_id": "oad_data_dictionary",
      "heading": "ObsAwareData data dictionary > 5. Measures",
      "text": "| `feature_name` | Source | Pathogens | Unit | `definition_status` | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `ed_visit_percent` | NSSP | covid, influenza, rsv, combined | `percent_ed_visits` | confirmed | Percent of all ED visits in the region and week that match the pathogen's syndrome definition. |\n| `ed_visit_percent_smoothed` | NSSP | covid, influenza, rsv, combined | `percent_ed_visits` | confirmed | CDC's smoothed version of the same percent. |\n| `ed_trend_category` | NSSP | covid, influenza, rsv | `categorical_trend` | uncertain | CDC's trend label: `Increasing`, `Decreasing`, `No Change`, or a data-availability note: `Data Unavailable`, `Limited Data`, `Sparse`, `No Data`. |"
    }
  ]
